Subject: [PATCH 3/3] termio/Exec: retain Darwin child wait status
Origin: QuickTTY local fix (not an upstream backport)
Base-Commit: 332b2aefc6e72d363aa93ab6ecfc86eeeeb5ed28 (v1.3.1)

The pinned libxev ProcessKqueue.wait reaps the child but discards the raw
wait status; its earlier kqueue result loses signal termination. Register
the same low-level proc completion on Darwin's static kqueue backend so
Exec owns the single reap. Preserve EXITSTATUS or 128 + TERMSIG, reporting
u32 max for unavailable/unrepresentable status, never manufacturing success.
A watcher error does not invalidate a subsequently collected raw status.

A single PID-specific WNOHANG probe distinguishes a terminal wait result
(or ECHILD, reported as unknown) from a live/unconfirmed child. Only the
former calls processExitCommon. On zero, EINTR, another wait error, or a
nonterminal status, retain cleanup ownership and disarm the proc watcher.
The existing termios timer then probes once per tick, using 250 ms while
fallback is pending; an already-active timer supplies the first tick.
Restart an inactive timer after the fault and keep it ticking even when
unfocused. Never re-register the failed process completion in a retry loop.

Source lifecycle review: Exec starts this timer on non-Windows targets;
focus loss normally stops it on its next tick rather than canceling it.
Thread.threadMain_ runs callbacks on the IO loop, then calls threadExit
before ThreadData.deinit. Cancellation permanently disables observation;
threadExit disables it before subprocess cleanup, and deinit repeats that
guard. Neither path probes, notifies, or restarts observation. A terminal
probe clears fallback before notifying and sets the existing exited flag,
so further callbacks cannot reap twice and cleanup skips the consumed PID.
ECHILD also releases ownership, avoiding later cleanup of a reused PID.
A live child remains owned by the unchanged subprocess shutdown path.

Source cases: normal exit 0/7 retains 0/7; signal exit retains 128 + signal.
Watcher failure while live emits nothing and continues timer observation;
failure after exit retains the raw status, or unknown on ECHILD. Interrupted
or failed probes defer to a later tick without blocking or an EINTR loop.
Other platforms retain the high-level watcher and original timer behavior.

No launch mode, C ABI, dependency, or PTY reader changes. The prior revision
passed git apply --check as reported by the controller, but native compilation
failed on a trailing statement semicolon in processExitDarwin. This revision
removes that semicolon; it has not been compiler-checked or runtime-tested.
Normal nonzero status propagation through login, cancellation/fault timing,
and final-tail PTY EOF ordering remain runtime gates; this is not a general
upstream fix.
Ghostty's MIT license applies; registration mirrors the pinned MIT-licensed
libxev ProcessKqueue.wait without modifying that dependency.

